    "c4"
   ],
   "spoken": "King's Indian or Nimzo-Indian? The Nimzo flies under the radar and is a superb positional choice.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "Nimzo as a positional weapon"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 37,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/4pn2/8/2PP4/8/PP2PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Let's play positionally — e6, heading for the Nimzo.",
   "kind": "main"
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 54,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/pppp1ppp/4pn2/8/2PP4/4P3/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "e3"
   ],
   "spoken": "One misconception: the Nimzo proper is only the knight to c3 met by the bishop to b4. Against anything else we shift to a Queen's-Indian or Bogo setup. White's e3 is passive but solid — it does box in his own bishop, and the ideal companion here is the Queen's-Indian plan with b6.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "when it is really the Nimzo"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 104.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/p1pp1ppp/1p2pn2/8/2PP4/4P3/PP3PPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "b6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Why not check on b4 now? The bishop check is most effective specifically against the knight once it commits to c3; here White just interposes on d2 and we lose the structure-wrecking capture. So b6 instead — d5 transposing into a favourable Queen's Gambit Declined was also fine.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "save Bb4 for Nc3"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 108.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/p1pp1ppp/1p2pn2/8/2PP4/4PN2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 111.5,
   "fen": "rn1qkb1r/pbpp1ppp/1p2pn2/8/2PP4/4PN2/PP3PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 5",
   "line": [
    "Bb7"
   ],
   "spoken": "We fianchetto the bishop, and note the lovely control it gives us over the e4-square.",
   "kind": "main",
   "teaches": [
    "fianchetto to fight for e4"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 114.5,
   "fen": "rn1qkb1r/pbpp1ppp/1p2pn2/8/2PP4/4PN2/PP2BPPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 5",
   "line": [
    "Be2"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 167.5,
   "fen": "rn1qkb1r/pbp2ppp/1p2pn2/3p4/2PP4/4PN2/PP2BPPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 6",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now d5 to stake out the centre — a chance to explain the pawn as an extension of the bishop. It looks like d5 buries our own bishop, and it does restrict it for the moment. But the aim is control of central squares: if we later plant a piece on e4 and recapture with a pawn, the bishop ends up defending that pawn — and we can always take on c4 to reopen the diagonal. No rush; complete development first.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "teaches": [
    "the pawn as an extension of the bishop"
   ]
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 170,
   "fen": "rn1qkb1r/pbp2ppp/1p2pn2/3p4/2PP4/4PN2/PP2BPPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 1 6",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
