   "t": 317.5,
   "fen": "r2qr1k1/pbpn1ppp/1p1b1n2/3p4/3P4/2NBPN2/PP3PPP/R1BQR1K1 w - - 4 11",
   "line": [
    "Re8"
   ],
   "spoken": "Here's the strategic engine of the position. Rather than the passive a6, we answer the knight-to-b5 threat with c5, turning up the temperature in the centre — and if the knight does jump to b5 we simply tuck the bishop back to b8, keeping it on its beautiful diagonal. Recapture with the pawn to preserve our central integrity, and now a d4 break can open that bishop with devastating effect — this is potential energy, the bishop coiled behind its own pawn. The calculation lesson runs through everything that follows: don't cash in too soon. Grabbing on f3 was more complicated than it looked, so keep every piece maximally active and hunt for a real knockout instead. There's a Greek-gift sacrifice in the air — the bishop crashing on h2, the knight to g4, and a queen infiltration exploiting an undefended f2 — and an all-hands-on-deck effort where even the sleepy knight must join. Control the e2-square so the king can never make its dash to safety, and the net closes with a rook to e2 and mate. The theme is converting potential energy into kinetic: often the simplest continuation is strongest because it keeps all your trumps.",
   "kind": "plan",
